<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Sacred · c. 1260 · Europe</p><h1 class="sg-title">Pange lingua gloriosi corporis mysterium</h1><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A Latin hymn from the Middle Ages, said to be by Thomas Aquinas, sung in church processions at the feast of Corpus Christi.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>attributed to Thomas Aquinas</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>traditional (plainchant: a Phrygian-mode tune of the Roman liturgy, and a second tune of the Mozarabic liturgy)</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>the Latin Church; later the Roman Catholic Church worldwide</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Latin</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>13th century</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Sacred</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (medieval Latin words attributed to Thomas Aquinas (1225–1274))</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pange-lingua-gloriosi-corporis-mysterium">Pange lingua gloriosi corporis mysterium</a>&quot; is a medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> hymn attributed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-aquinas">Thomas Aquinas</a> (1225–1274), written for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corpus-christi-feast">Feast of Corpus Christi</a>. It is also sung on Maundy Thursday, during the procession that carries the Blessed Sacrament from the church to the place where it is kept until Good Friday. Its opening words echo an older and famous Latin hymn, &quot;Pange lingua gloriosi proelium certaminis&quot; by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venantius-fortunatus">Venantius Fortunatus</a>, from which it is derived.</p><p>The hymn sets out the Catholic doctrine that the bread and wine are changed into the body and blood of Christ during the Mass. Its last two stanzas, known by their opening words &quot;Tantum ergo&quot;, are often sung on their own: at Benediction of the Blessed Sacrament, and at the end of the procession of the monstrance on Holy Thursday. In English it is often sung as &quot;Of the Glorious Body Telling&quot;, to the same tune as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>, and Edward Caswall made one of its many English translations.</p><p>Two plainchant tunes carry it. The better known is a tune in the Phrygian mode (Mode III) from the Roman liturgy, which was first part of the Gallican Rite; the other comes from the Mozarabic liturgy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. Both became the ground of later polyphony. Juan de Urrede, a Flemish composer working in Spain in the late 15th century, wrote numerous settings, most of them on the Mozarabic melody; one of his four-voice versions was among the most popular pieces of the 16th century and was the basis for dozens of keyboard works and masses. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josquin-des-prez">Josquin des Prez</a> built his Missa Pange lingua, one of his last works, dated to between 1515 and 1521 and published after his death, on the Roman tune; many commentators count it among the high points of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> polyphony.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josquin-des-prez">Josquin</a>'s treatment of the hymn's third line in the Kyrie of that mass gave music a theme of six notes that composers took up for centuries, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johann-sebastian-bach">Johann Sebastian Bach</a> and Johann Fux, whose fugues on it in the Gradus ad Parnassum made it known to every student of composition, Mozart included. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anton-bruckner">Anton Bruckner</a>'s first composition set the hymn's first strophe. The Tantum ergo has its own settings, by Palestrina, Schubert, Bruckner (eight of them), Duruflé and Widor. An organ reharmonization of the hymn by Beethoven was found by the musicologist Barry Cooper in October 2012.</p><p>The hymn has been translated into many languages for worship, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> remains the most sung. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syriac">Syriac</a> translation was used at Benediction in the Syro-Malabar Catholic Church of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerala">Kerala</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, until the 1970s. By a decree of 25 August 1818 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-pius-vii">Pope Pius VII</a> attached an indulgence of 300 days to each recitation of the whole hymn, followed by its customary verse, response and prayer, and of 100 days to the Tantum ergo alone.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A hymn of praise for the sacrament of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eucharist">Eucharist</a>, expressing the belief that in the Mass the bread and wine become the body and blood of Christ. Its last two stanzas turn to adoration of the sacrament. The mood is solemn and reverent.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">Latin</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-2"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">Latin</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Pange, lingua, gloriosi</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Sing, tongue, the mystery of the glorious</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">corporis mysterium,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>body</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">sanguinisque pretiosi,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and of the precious blood</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">quem in mundi pretium</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>which, as the ransom of the world,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">fructus ventris generosi</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>the fruit of a noble womb,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">rex effudit gentium.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>the king of the nations, poured out.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Nobis datus, nobis natus</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Given to us, born for us</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">ex intacta Virgine,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>of an untouched Virgin,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">et in mundo conversatus,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and having lived in the world,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">sparso verbi semine,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>the seed of the word sown,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">sui moras incolatus</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>the days of his dwelling here</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">miro clausit ordine.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>he closed in a wondrous order.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">In supremae nocte coenae</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>On the night of the last supper,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">recumbens cum fratribus,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>reclining with the brothers,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">observata lege plene</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>the law fully kept</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">cibis in legalibus,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>in the foods the law prescribed,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">cibum turbae duodenae</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>as food to the band of twelve</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">se dat suis manibus.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>he gives himself with his own hands.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Verbum caro panem verum</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The Word made flesh makes true bread</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">verbo carnem efficit,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>into flesh by a word,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">fitque sanguis Christi merum;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and the wine becomes the blood of Christ;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">et si sensus deficit,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and if the senses fail,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">ad firmandum cor sincerum</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>to make firm a sincere heart</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">sola fides sufficit.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>faith alone suffices.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Tantum ergo sacramentum</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>So great a sacrament, therefore,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">veneremur cernui,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>let us venerate with bowed heads,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">et antiquum documentum</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and let the ancient lesson</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">novo cedat ritui,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>give way to the new rite;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">praestet fides supplementum</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>let faith supply what is lacking</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">sensuum defectui.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>where the senses fall short.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Genitori Genitoque,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>To the Begetter and the Begotten</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">laus et iubilatio,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>be praise and jubilation,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">salus, honor, virtus quoque</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>salvation, honour, and power too,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">sit et benedictio.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and blessing;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Procedenti ab utroque</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>to the one proceeding from both</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">compar sit laudatio.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>be equal praise.</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Attributed to Thomas Aquinas (13th century). Text from <a href="https://la.wikisource.org/wiki/Pange_lingua" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a>; public domain.</p><p>The English is the Institute&#8217;s own translation, made from the original for this page.</p></div><details class="sg-notes"><summary>Notes on the translation (5)</summary><ul><li>line 1.4: 'pretium' is 'price', the price paid for the world.</li><li>line 1.5-6: 'fructus ventris generosi' stands beside 'rex gentium': the king of the nations, the fruit of a noble womb, poured out the blood.</li><li>line 4.1-2: 'Verbum caro' may be read as one phrase, 'the Word made flesh', or 'Word, flesh' in apposition; the line plays on verbum (word) and caro (flesh).</li><li>line 5.3: 'documentum' is 'teaching, example', here the rites of the old law.</li><li>line 6.5: 'the one proceeding from both' is the Holy Spirit.</li></ul></details></section>
</div>
