<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired"><h2>What it inspired</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Of the Glorious Body Telling (Edward Caswall)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">English translation, sung to the same tune</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Settings of the Pange lingua (Juan de Urrede)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">late 15th century</span><span class="sg-chain-h">settings, most on the Mozarabic melody</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Missa Pange lingua (Josquin des Prez)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1515–1521</span><span class="sg-chain-h">mass built on the Roman tune</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Fugues on the Pange lingua theme (Johann Fux, in Gradus ad Parnassum)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">fugues on Josquin's theme</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Symphony No. 41, &quot;Jupiter&quot; (Wolfgang Amadeus Mozart)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">its theme borrows the first four notes of Josquin's theme</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Tantum ergo (Giovanni Pierluigi da Palestrina)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">setting of the last two stanzas</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Tantum ergo (Franz Schubert)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">setting of the last two stanzas</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Pange lingua, WAB 31 (Anton Bruckner)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">setting of the first strophe; his first composition</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Eight settings of the Tantum ergo (Anton Bruckner)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">settings of the last two stanzas</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Tantum ergo (Maurice Duruflé)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">setting of the last two stanzas</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Tantum ergo (Charles-Marie Widor)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">setting of the last two stanzas</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Organ reharmonization of Pange lingua (Ludwig van Beethoven)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">reharmonization; found in 2012</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Night Procession, from Two Episodes from Lenau's Faust (Franz Liszt)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">largely a fantasy on the melody</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Pange lingua (Ciaran McLoughlin), on Solas An Domhain (Solas)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1995</span><span class="sg-chain-h">setting</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Syriac translation (Syro-Malabar Catholic Church)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">translation, used at Benediction until the 1970s</span></li></ul></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired-by"><h2>What it drew on</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Pange lingua gloriosi proelium certaminis (Venantius Fortunatus)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">the hymn from which it is derived; its opening words are echoed</span></li></ul></section>
